      "text": "To find the ratio of the shorter height to the original height, we start by using the formula for the volume of a cone, which is given by:\n\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( r \\) is the radius of the base and \\( h \\) is the height of the cone.\n\n### Step 1: Find the original height\nGiven the original height is 30 inches, we calculate the radius using the circumference formula \\( C = 2 \\pi r \\):\n\n\\[\nC = 16 \\pi\n\\]\n\n\\[\n2 \\pi r = 16 \\pi\n\\]\n\n\\[\nr = \\frac{16 \\pi}{2 \\pi} = 8 \\, \\text{inches}\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Find the new circumference\nThe circumference remains the same, so:\n\n\\[\nC = 16 \\pi \\, \\text{inches}\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Find the new radius\nThe circumference formula is:\n\n\\[\nC = 2 \\pi r\n\\]\n\n\\[\n16 \\pi = 2 \\pi r\n\\]\n\n\\[\nr = \\frac{16 \\pi}{2 \\pi} = 8 \\, \\text{inches}\n\\]\n\n### Step 4: Use the volume formula again with the new circumference\nWe know the volume of the shorter cone:\n\n\\[\nV = 192 \\pi \\, \\text{cubic inches}\n\\]\n\nThe volume formula is:\n\n\\[\n192 \\pi = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h_{\\text{short}}\n\\]\n\n### Step 5: Solve for \\( h_{\\text{short}} \\)\nSubstitute \\( r = 8 \\):\n\n\\[\n192 \\pi = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (8)^2 h_{\\text{short}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n192 \\pi = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (64) h_{\\text{short}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n192 \\pi = \\frac{64}{3} \\pi h_{\\text{short}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n192 = \\frac{64}{3} h_{\\text{short}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n192 \\times 3 = 64 h_{\\text{short}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n576 = 64 h_{\\text{short}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nh_{\\text{short}} = \\frac{576}{64} = 9 \\, \\text{inches}\n\\]\n\n### Step 6: Find the ratio of the shorter height to the original height\nThe original height is 30 inches and the shorter height is 9 inches. The ratio is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{h_{\\text{short}}}{h_{\\text{original}}} = \\frac{9}{30} = \\frac{3}{10}\n\\]\n\nThus, the ratio of the shorter height to the original height is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3}{10}}\n\\]",
